-- ============ A6. MY R14 DIAGNOSIS IN BATCH 3 WAS WRONG ============
UPDATE search_executions SET
  findings_note = findings_note || ' || ADVERSARIAL CORRECTION 2026-07-26: THE R14 DIAGNOSIS IN THIS ROW IS WRONG. I recorded that "PubMed returned a single unrelated Holden+Fernie record: late-1980s ergonomics is thinly indexed there, a WRONG-INDEX result rather than absence." Both claims are false. ALL FOUR of the batch-3 primaries are indexed in PubMed - PMIDs 15676714, 15676669, 11276262, 10855457 - and the single record my query returned (15676669) WAS one of my two targets, the 1988 paper. I did not check the returned PMID against what I was looking for, and then blamed the index. The true diagnosis is QUERY SHAPE PLUS MY OWN SCREENING FAILURE. R14 exists to stop exactly this: misattributing a retrieval failure to the wrong cause makes the next session distrust a database that was working.'
WHERE session='session_2026-07-26-energy-conservation-rest-points-seating-b3'
  AND query_text LIKE 'Crossref query.bibliographic: Holden Fernie%';

